    Aerodynamics
<h3>The 4 forces of flight</h3>
<p>The 4 forces of flight are weight, lift, drag, and thrust. These 4 forces control the details of flight, but if we can control the forces we can control flight.</p>
<h3>Weight</h3>
<p>Everything has weight. Weight is the opposite of lift. A plane needs weight to land. The weight of an object controls how strong the push is.</p>
<h3>Lift</h3>
<p>Lift is the force that makes the plane go up. It is the opposite of weight. something that is curved can cause lift. The things that have lift such as hot air, rotor blades, and wings. Lift controls how much the plane’s pushed upwards.</p>
<h3>Drag</h3>
<p>	Drag tries to slow things down. It is the opposite of thrust. Round surfaces have less drag than flat surfaces, and narrow surfaces have less drag than wide surfaces. The more air that hits a surface, the more drag there is.</p>
<h3>Thrust</h3>
<p>Thrust is the force that moves something forward. Thrust is the opposite of drag. Airplanes have more thrust than drag. A small airplane gets thrust from	its propellers. A large airplane gets thrust from its jet engines. A glider doesn’t have thrust, it can only fly until drag slows it down.</p>
